Short:        Text editor for programmers on OS 3.2
Author:       André Gewert <agewert@ubergeek.de>
Uploader:     André Gewert <agewert@ubergeek.de>
Type:         text/edit
Version:      1.0
Architecture: m68k-amigaos >= 3.2.0
Requires:     AmigaOS 3.2, dev/gui/html_gadget.lha (1.2, for the help)
Distribution: Aminet

TinyDev is a compact native text editor for programmers on AmigaOS 3.2,
built with ReAction. It runs on a 68000, but is meant for a 68030 or
faster.

TinyDev is meant to be run on real hardware, not only in emulators and on
PiStorm machines. Benchmark is my good old Amiga 2000, equipped with
a TF356 (68030, 64MB RAM) and a GBAPII++.

In the future, TinyDev aims to be a simple but complete development
environment for AmigaOS 3.2. It supports multiple programming languages
through a simple extendable JSON-based protocol. External build tools
(like make or smake) can be integrated through configurable commands.

The user interface supports localization via AmigaOS catalogs. Currently,
next to built-in English texts, a German catalog is included.

As you can guess, most of the code is AI-generated (using different models
at the beginning; now Claude Opus 5.5 only). I will try to manually check
and improve the generated code.

Features
--------
 - ReAction-based user interface with support for translations
 - Settings per file type
 - Integrated directory browser
 - Minimap: a coloured overview of the document; click or drag in it to
   scroll.
 - Simple project management
 - File and project templates
 - Syntax highlighting for various languages
 - Holstein, a monospaced bitmap font for the screen (sizes 16 and 17,
   derived from DejaVu Sans Mono)
 - Bookmark management
 - Static source code analysis
 - Symbol list
 - Auto-completion based on the symbols found
   (NOT a simple keyword list)
 - Find and replace (Amiga+F) in multiple documents; supports regular
   expressions
 - Integrated documentation (work in progress)
    - The HelpViewer is a simple but functional tool for displaying the
      supplied documentation
    - TinyDev includes a project template and all the tools needed to
      create and edit your own help pages
 - Extensibility without changing TinyDev:
    - You can add your own templates
    - You can add your own source code parsers
 - Reusable components:
    - The ReAction classes dirtree and minimap can be used in your own
      ReAction applications
    - The source code analysis tools (Scout) are designed so that they can
      be integrated into your own applications through a simple protocol
      (ARexx and JSON)
    - The HelpViewer is designed as a general-purpose tool. Its license
      allows it to be embedded in other projects

Installation
------------
Double-click Install: everything goes into one drawer, so deleting the
drawer removes TinyDev. For an update, choose the same place; your settings
stay. TinyDev also runs directly from the archive, as it finds all its
parts next to it. html.gadget must be installed in SYS:Classes/Gadgets/ or
lie in the drawer Classes/Gadgets of TinyDev.

Contents
--------
 Install              the installer script
 TinyDev              the editor
 TinyDev-Scout        source analysis commodity (ARexx port TINYDEV_SCOUT)
 parsers/             Scout's parsers: C, scripts, Makefiles, Markdown,
                      JSON, HTML, XML, ARexx, Assembler, AmigaE, ACE, Pascal,
                      AmigaGuide
 HelpViewer           the help viewer
 Sprout               creates projects from templates (Shell)
 Templates/           the templates of new projects and files
 mdtohtml             Markdown to HTML, also whole drawers (help pages)
 Help/                help pages (english, deutsch)
 Classes/Gadgets/     dirtree.gadget and minimap.gadget
 Catalogs/            translations (German)
 Fonts/               Holstein, a monospaced bitmap font (sizes 16, 17),
                      with its licence
 Docs/                documentation (README.md)
 Icons/               the archive's icons as Classic, GlowIcons and
                      NewIcons (and scripts that put them in place)
 Source/              complete source code (C, cross compiled with
                      bebbo's amiga-gcc)

